{"id": "tea:yogasikha-upanisad:3.2-10", "source": "src:yogasikha-upanisad", "location": {"ref": "3.2-10"}, "paraphrase": "The power in the mūlādhāra as bindu sprouts as nāda (paśyantī), roars in the heart (madhyamā) and comes forth by the palate as vaikharī; from it arise letters, words, sentences, all mantras, Vedas, śāstras, purāṇas, poetry, languages and the seven notes; she is Sarasvatī; seeing her gives mastery of speech.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["sound", "meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["sound-language"], "concepts": ["cpt:levels-of-speech"], "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": [], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [{"phase": "C", "date": "2026-09-29", "method": "text-locate", "queries": ["e-text yogasikha-upanisad 3.2-10"], "evidence": ["catalog:raw_etexts:108_Upanishads", "text:sources_raw/raw_etexts/vedaH/misc/upaniShat/mixedPF/108_Upanishads.md"], "result": "confirmed", "note": "E-text 3.2-10 located; the passage matches the paraphrase (it begins: 'akṣaraṃ paramo nādaḥ śabdabrahmeti kathyate | mūlādhāra-gatā śaktiḥ svādhārā bindurūpiṇī || 2 ||…').", "by": "sourcing:U04-minor-upanisads"}]}, "coverage": ["A2"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U04-minor-upanisads"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasikha-upanisad:4", "source": "src:yogasikha-upanisad", "location": {"ref": "4"}, "paraphrase": "Non-dual teaching: the world is superimposed like a snake on a rope; one who makes even a little difference has fear.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "absolute", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["ultimate"], "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": [], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [{"phase": "C", "date": "2026-09-29", "method": "text-locate", "queries": ["e-text yogasikha-upanisad 4"], "evidence": ["catalog:raw_etexts:108_Upanishads", "text:sources_raw/raw_etexts/vedaH/misc/upaniShat/mixedPF/108_Upanishads.md"], "result": "confirmed", "note": "Ch. 4 of the e-text opens with the rope-and-snake verse (4.1-2). V. 4.8 says that whoever puts even a little difference between jīvātman and paramātman has fear (svalpam apy antaraṃ kṛtvā … bhayaṃ tasyāpi bhāṣitam).", "by": "sourcing:U04-minor-upanisads"}]}, "coverage": ["A2"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U04-minor-upanisads"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasikha-upanisad:5.46-55", "source": "src:yogasikha-upanisad", "location": {"ref": "5.46-55"}, "paraphrase": "Concentration on nāda gives far-hearing, on bindu far-seeing, on kāla knowledge of the three times, on another's body entry into bodies, on nectar freedom from hunger and poison, on the elements their mastery; bhāva is the cause.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["body-breath", "meditation"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["powers-experiences"], "concepts": ["cpt:siddhis-in-yoga-upanisads"], "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": [], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": [{"phase": "C", "date": "2026-09-29", "method": "text-locate", "queries": ["e-text yogasikha-upanisad 5.46-55"], "evidence": ["catalog:raw_etexts:108_Upanishads", "text:sources_raw/raw_etexts/vedaH/misc/upaniShat/mixedPF/108_Upanishads.md"], "result": "confirmed", "note": "E-text 5.46-55 are located: nāde manolayaḥ gives dūraśravaṇa; bindu gives dūradarśana; kālātman gives trikālajñāna; parakāyapraveśa; amṛta in the head; the elements.", "by": "sourcing:U04-minor-upanisads"}]}, "coverage": ["A2"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U04-minor-upanisads"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:yogasikha-upanisad:5.56-59", "source": "src:yogasikha-upanisad", "location": {"ref": "5.56-59"}, "paraphrase": "The guru is Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Sadāśiva; there is no difference between guru and Lord; one should never argue non-duality against the guru, but contemplate with devotion the non-duality of guru, god and self.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["teacher-transmission"], "concepts": ["cpt:guru"], "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": [], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "sourced", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": [{"phase": "C", "date": "2026-09-29", "method": "text-locate", "queries": ["e-text yogasikha-upanisad 5.56-59"], "evidence": ["catalog:raw_etexts:108_Upanishads", "text:sources_raw/raw_etexts/vedaH/misc/upaniShat/mixedPF/108_Upanishads.md"], "result": "confirmed", "note": "E-text 5.56-59 located; the passage matches the paraphrase (it begins: 'gururbrahmā gururviṣṇurgururdevaḥ sadāśivaḥ | naguroradhikaḥ kaścittriṣu lokeṣu vidyate || 56 ||…').", "by": "sourcing:U04-minor-upanisads"}]}, "coverage": ["A2"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U04-minor-upanisads"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
